Immigration detention · October 1, 2026 · updated 2026-10-03
The Supreme Court agreed to decide whether people already in the country who were never admitted must be held without a bond hearing
The Court's October 1 order takes the case and decides nothing yet. For about thirty years, people arrested inside the country who had entered without inspection and had not been admitted or paroled could ask an immigration judge for release on bond, unless they were apprehended while entering or shortly afterward or held on a criminal or terrorism ground. Since July 2025 the government has said they must be held, and the appeals courts have split.
30 years Practice of letting these detainees ask for bond, by the Second Circuit
156 days From the Second Circuit's April 28 decision to the grant
The grant
What happened
On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court agreed to decide whether the government must hold, while their removal cases are pending, people who are living in the United States and were never admitted. The order takes the case; it does not decide it. Two laws are in play. One, called here the detention statute, says that in the case of an applicant for admission, a person seeking admission "shall be detained" if an officer determines that person is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, except for a crewman, a stowaway, or a person an earlier provision of the detention statute already covers, and except that a person arriving by land from a contiguous territory may be returned there instead. The other, the bond statute, says the government "may release" a person on bond.
For about thirty years, by the Second Circuit's account, people who entered without inspection and were not admitted or paroled, and who were not apprehended while entering or shortly afterward or held on a criminal or terrorism ground, could ask for bond. On July 10, 2025, a memorandum from the commissioner of Customs and Border Protection said the detention statute covers them instead. On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals said immigration judges lack authority to grant them bond. On September 26, 2025, officers arrested Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts; an immigration judge denied him a bond hearing, a district court ordered a bond hearing or release within ten days, and an immigration judge released him on bond after finding no danger and no flight risk. On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit ruled the other way from the Board in Barbosa da Cunha's case. On October 1 the Supreme Court agreed to decide the question.
The detention statute uses two descriptions of the person, an applicant for admission and an alien seeking admission. It tells an immigration officer to detain that person for a removal proceeding when the officer decides the person seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.
Detention statute · 2024 compilation · Inspection of applicants for admission
in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained
Government Publishing Office
The Second Circuit held that Barbosa da Cunha may be an applicant for admission but is not seeking admission, because he is not requesting lawful entry after inspection and authorization; he was already living here. So the court held that the detention statute does not apply to someone in the United States after entering without inspection and admission who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter. The Second Circuit affirmed the district court, which had required a bond hearing or release. Under the July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Parole is a separate power. The parole statute lets the government release a person "only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit". The July 10 memorandum says the only people eligible for release on bond are people already admitted.
U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit · April 28, 2026 · Opinion
Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”
Barbosa da Cunha
Barbosa da Cunha entered around 2005, lived in the United States for more than twenty years, applied for asylum in 2016, and held a work permit. He has never been arrested for or charged with a crime. If the government wins, people in his situation cannot be released on bond: the Second Circuit wrote that if the detention statute applies, he "must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond". By September 10 the question had reached every numbered federal appeals court. Nine, including the Second and the Fourth, are the courts the Fourth Circuit said had held that the bond statute applies to noncitizens inside the country, like the people who filed the cases, and that they must be given a bond hearing. The Second Circuit's own holding covers people who entered without inspection and were not apprehended while entering or shortly afterward. Two, the Fifth and the Eighth, held that the detention statute applies to everyone who has not been admitted.
The government's petition asked the Court to wait on this case. It said the Court should hold this petition pending another petition, Lopez-Campos, which it called a "better vehicle" for resolving whether holding those people is lawful, and then dispose of this petition as appropriate. The October 1 order granted this petition.
The catch
Tens of thousands of immigrants
CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants during removal proceedings without bond hearings. No court or agency record gives a precise count of people held under the government's reading. The Fourth Circuit says the petitioners, the people who brought the cases, are among thousands of noncitizens swept up and detained without bond, and that district courts sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases, which counts court cases, not people in detention.
Who the policy reaches
Customs and Border Protection's public notice describes mandatory detention for applicants for admission the Department of Homeland Security encounters for the first time after they fail to lawfully present for inspection at a port of entry. The July 10 memorandum says the first encounter counts "regardless of when or where that first encounter occurs". So the policy reaches a person arrested inside the country years after entering: Barbosa da Cunha was arrested driving to work in Norwood more than twenty years after he entered.
The practice
Where this sits
The 1997 Federal Register rule made people present without having been admitted or paroled eligible for bond. The grant came 156 days after the Second Circuit's decision.
Date
Record
March 6, 1997
A Federal Register rule says people present without admission will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.
July 8, 2025
The appeals opinion cites immigration enforcement guidance on detention of applicants for admission.
July 10, 2025
Customs and Border Protection says its commissioner issued detention guidance to every component.
Sept. 5, 2025
The Board of Immigration Appeals decides judges lack authority to grant bond in this situation.
Sept. 26, 2025
Officers arrested the man in this case while he was driving to work.
Apr. 28, 2026
The Second Circuit affirms the order requiring a bond hearing or release.
Sept. 10, 2026
The Fourth Circuit names the appeals courts that require a bond hearing and the two that do not.
Sept. 25, 2026
The Second Circuit denies rehearing by the full court.
Oct. 1, 2026
The Supreme Court grants review in this case.
On March 6, 1997, a Federal Register rule, effective April 1, said people present without having been admitted or paroled would be eligible for bond. The Second Circuit counts Executive Branch practice over thirty years and across five administrations, and it says the Justice Department understood the detention statute the same way when it issued that rule. On September 25, 2026, the full Second Circuit refused to rehear the case. Judge Steven J. Menashi dissented, arguing that the detention statute applies and that Barbosa da Cunha must be detained without a bond hearing. Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted against rehearing while writing that he agreed with the dissent's analysis.
Open questions
What we do not know yet
No argument date has been set. The Court took one question, what the detention statute means. Whether holding someone without a bond hearing violates the Constitution's guarantee of due process is not part of it; the government's petition says a second case, Lopez-Campos from the Sixth Circuit, raises that question as well. The Court had not granted or denied review in that case or in a third, Buenrostro-Mendez from the Fifth Circuit, as of October 3. A motion to extend the time to respond was granted in Lopez-Campos, and Buenrostro-Mendez was rescheduled. Both were sent to the same September 28 conference.
