data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt-500-6 The majority adopts the Tenth Circuit’s assertion that Section 1225(a)(5) shows “‘that applicants for admission can be seeking admission, not that they must be.’” Maj. Op. 29 n.9 (quoting Santillan Quiroz v. Mullin, 180 F.4th 1226, 1245 (10th Cir. 2026)). That conclusion, however, is based not on the text of the statute but on a hypothetical sentence that does not parallel Section 1225(a)(5)’s structure because it omits the “regarding” clause and, particularly, the use of a definite article to refer back to “the applicant.” See Santillan Quiroz, 180 F.4th at 1245.
