## First read

The page told me that the Supreme Court took a dispute over bond hearings for people arrested inside the country long after entering without admission, following a change in government practice.

I still wanted to know why the government preferred another case and whether the grant changed anyone’s custody or access to a hearing now. I first lost the thread at: “One, called here the detention statute,” as the sentence moved into exceptions before explaining the dispute.

## The story

The record supports this story: a [1997 rule](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06.txt) said people present without admission “will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination”; the [July 2025 memorandum](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt) assigned them to “section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236”; Barbosa da Cunha obtained a bond hearing and release after his arrest; and the Supreme Court [granted review](https://www.supremecourt.gov/orders/courtorders/100126zr_6j37.pdf) of the statutory question on October 1, 2026.

**The event has not moved beyond the grant in the primary dockets checked on October 3.** The [case docket](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-104.html) ends its proceedings with “Petition GRANTED.” and an October 1 instruction for later electronic filings. The [Lopez-Campos](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/25-1415.html) and [Buenrostro-Mendez](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-43.html) dockets show no later disposition. The [ACLU’s October 1 statement](https://www.aclu.org/press-releases/aclu-comment-on-supreme-court-agreeing-to-hear-challenge-over-trump-mandatory-detention-policy), checked as an actor’s primary statement, concerns the grant.

The page has the right angle and opening act. One passage near the top gives the dates in order: changed position, Board decision, arrest and denied hearing, release, appeals ruling, grant. Its 56-word arrest sentence makes the consequence harder to retain. The unresolved issue is stated accurately. The page spends its opening space on statutory exceptions before showing why the distinction matters.

| Answer | Does the marked paragraph carry it? |
| --- | --- |
| 1. What happened | **Yes.** It states the October 1 grant and says the order does not decide the case. |
| 2. Why it matters | **Yes, late.** The paragraph gives his circumstances, loss of bond under the government’s reading, and the wider split. The early sequence supplies the actual hearing and release. Move the consequence into a shorter early sentence; do not add another account. |
| 3. Easiest wrong reading | **Partly.** The marked paragraph distinguishes court cases from people detained, but its heading first presents the outlet’s number as the page’s own. It needs the correction beside that heading. |
| 4. Mechanism | **With effort.** The paragraph gives the court’s distinction and the parole alternative. Ask for one plain sentence connecting the statutory label to his presence inside the country, in place of the opening exceptions. |
| 5. What changed | **Not as marked.** The marked paragraph gives the 1997 rule and rehearing dispute. The early dated passage carries the intervening change, Board decision, appeal and grant. Keep that sequence; trim the later retelling. |
| 6. Unresolved | **Yes.** It separates this statutory grant from due process and says no argument day is set. The other petitions’ procedural details obscure that answer. |

